WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Templates are one of the most powerful features in C++.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:11.160
They let libraries and applications express a generic operation once and reuse it across many types.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.540
That reuse can reduce duplication, improve consistency, and make APIs more expressive.

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.920
It also means the behavior that finally runs may depend on the type used to instantiate the template.

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.360
A template that looks safe with one type may be incorrect with another.

6
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:32.620
The type may supply different overloads, traits, policies, comparators, allocators, or conversion behavior.

7
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.160
Build configuration can also influence which specializations are present and which diagnostics are enabled.

8
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:43.140
Generic code therefore has security-relevant assumptions even when the template body looks small.

9
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.380
Template security is not about avoiding templates.

10
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.760
It is about making generic assumptions visible before they surprise a caller or reviewer.

11
00:00:51.860 --> 00:01:01.220
A secure generic API describes what kinds of types are intended, what operations may be called, what ownership rules apply, and how invalid use fails.

12
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.580
Some defects appear only in specific instantiations.

13
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:13.120
A happy-path test with one common type does not prove that every valid instantiation preserves the same invariants.

14
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:21.140
Defensive engineering includes representative tests, useful compile-time errors, clear constraints, and review of customization points.

15
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.540
The practical goal is disciplined generic design.

16
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:33.360
Templates should make intended use clear, reject unintended use early, and keep type-dependent behavior understandable enough for security review.

17
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:43.720
A useful review habit is to ask what would happen if the next caller supplied a move-only type, a throwing type, a custom allocator, or a user-defined operation.

18
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.460
That question quickly reveals whether the template contract is explicit enough.
